<DocsPage id="cloud-services">
  <p>
    Cloud Services connects the repositories you work on to your own Vercel and Supabase accounts. Resources remain
    in those accounts, with their own billing, quotas and terms. Free model inference does not make hosting or
    database operations free.
  </p>
  <p class="note">
    The connection, environment and publishing improvements below arrived in desktop 1.99.99. Hosted provider
    sign-in is not enabled yet; use the manual token option in Connections where your desktop version supports it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="connect-accounts">Connect an account and select a project</h2>
  <p>
    Open Cloud Services → Connections. When hosted sign-in is configured, finish approval in your browser. When it is
    unavailable, supported desktop builds offer a personal access token from the provider's settings, validated and
    encrypted on this device. Supabase setup from Database leads to the same connection flow. Vector verifies the
    account before showing it as connected.
  </p>
  <p>
    Link the existing Vercel project that this repository should use, or choose its Supabase project in Database.
    From 1.99.99, the desktop uses an encrypted temporary callback relay for Vercel, and PKCE for Supabase. Provider client secrets stay on the server. Management tokens are not returned to the renderer after saving.
  </p>

  <h2 id="project-environment">Manage project variables</h2>
  <p>
    Environment variable names keep the case you enter. Values are encrypted on your device and hidden by default, and
    drafts and revealed values clear when you change project or task. Each variable is public or server-only, and a
    server-only value can never use a browser-visible name such as VITE_ or NEXT_PUBLIC_. Add or remove values in Vector, then choose Write to .env to update its
    managed block. Applying an empty list clears that block after the final removal; your lines above the marker remain.
    The file is replaced atomically, symbolic links are rejected, and POSIX systems use private file permissions.
  </p>
  <p>
    For a detected or configured Vite project, Vector preserves literal dollar signs and backslashes through Vite's
    environment parser. Enter literal values rather than expansion expressions. Multiline values and unsupported quote
    combinations are rejected before the file changes; set those values directly in the provider's environment settings.
    Other frameworks retain their existing serialization.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sync to Vercel sends the configured values to the linked project. Local .env changes and remote provider
    values are separate: removing a local entry does not confirm remote deletion. Supabase setup adds public client
    values, including the VITE_SUPABASE_URL and VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY aliases used by the generated client. Secret,
    service-role, management and user-session keys are rejected for that browser configuration.
  </p>
  <p>
    A Vercel integration can manage the variables it owns. Pre-existing variables created outside that integration
    may need to be managed directly in Vercel. You can still list projects and manage or apply local variables in Vector.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cloud-actions">Run cloud work from a task</h2>
  <p>
    The agent's cloud tool can inspect connections, detect build settings, prepare a linked database or authentication,
    sync variables, publish and inspect recent deployments. It asks before it publishes, changes a database or writes a
    host's environment, and the prompt names the provider, project and environment. A wildcard allow does not skip
    these prompts; only a permission rule that names the cloud action does.
    Publishing goes to the Vercel project linked to the repository; without one, the tool returns setup guidance. A
    failed Vercel deployment never triggers a deployment somewhere else.
  </p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li><strong>Deployment checks and logs</strong><span>Publishing can run configured build, health and browser checks. Their results and provider logs show what was verified and what failed.</span></li>
    <li><strong>Database setup</strong><span>Reuse a linked Supabase project, or explicitly approve creation in a chosen organization. Provider quotas and billing still apply. Vector does not automatically upgrade plans or move creation to another organization after a limit.</span></li>
    <li><strong>Repository migrations</strong><span>Inspect a dry-run plan before applying .sql migrations to the linked database. Each file runs in one transaction with its record, which Vector keeps with a checksum in a private schema your app's API keys cannot reach. Files the Supabase CLI already applied are skipped, an applied file that was edited stops the run, and execution stops at the first failure.</span></li>
    <li><strong>Account control</strong><span>Disconnect removes Vector's local credential. Supabase OAuth revocation is attempted; local removal alone does not prove provider consent was revoked.</span></li>
  </ul>
  <p class="note">
    These actions run through the Vector desktop app, where provider connections live. The standalone terminal CLI
    reports the desktop cloud tool as unavailable.
  </p>
</DocsPage>
